-- Backfill `recipe`.`method` for Recipes imported from a URL.
--
-- Generated by scripts/backfill-recipe-method.mjs on 2026-08-06
-- against http://localhost:8080/.netlify/functions/recipes
-- by re-reading each Recipe's own remote_url through the app's URL Import path
-- and keeping only the method. Resolves follow-ups.md #41 for the Recipes that
-- have a source to re-read. Counts, and every skip with its reason, are at the
-- foot of this file.
--
-- Safe to apply more than once, and safe to apply to a database that has moved
-- on since this was generated. Every statement below:
--
--   * writes `method` and nothing else. Ingredients, name and tags are never
--     touched, so a page that re-read thinly cannot damage good data - #41's
--     worst case;
--   * requires `remote_url` still to match the page the method was read from,
--     so a Recipe whose id has shifted is a no-op rather than a wrong write;
--   * requires `method` still to be empty, so a method written by hand since
--     this file was generated is never overwritten.
--
-- Deliberately not wrapped in a transaction: each statement is independent and
-- self-guarding, so a partially written or partially applied file is still
-- correct. Re-run the generator to pick up whatever is still missing.
--
-- Pure DML. Unlike 027 this file is not endangered by `mysql --force`, since
-- every statement stands alone - a skipped one costs one method and nothing
-- else. It is applied that way locally, in fact: docker/mysql-init runs every
-- migrations/*.sql with --force, and this one matches no rows there at all
-- because migrations run against an empty database. Against production, apply
-- it without --force, so that a failure is visible rather than skipped.

-- Method text is full of degree signs, accents and dashes, and a mysql client
-- whose default character set is latin1 will read these UTF-8 bytes as latin1
-- and store them double-encoded - "180°C" arriving as "180Â°C" - without
-- erroring. Declaring the encoding here rather than relying on how the file
-- happens to be piped in is what stops that.
SET NAMES utf8mb4;

-- 1  Shepherds Pie
--     https://www.thecoast.net.nz/life/food-recipes/gordon-ramsay-s-shepherds-pie/
UPDATE `recipe` SET `method` = '1. Preheat the oven to 180°C.
2. Heat the oil in a large pan until hot. Season the lamb mince and fry over moderate-high heat for 2.5 minutes.
3. Stir in the onion and carrot, then grate in the garlic. Add the Worcestershire sauce, tomato puree, thyme and rosemary, and cook for 1.5 minutes, stirring constantly.
4. Pour in the red wine and reduce until almost completely evaporated. Add the chicken stock, bring to the boil, then simmer until the sauce has thickened.
5. Meanwhile, cook the potatoes in boiling salted water until tender. Drain, then return them to the hot pan over low heat briefly to dry out.
6. Pass the potatoes through a potato ricer, then beat in the egg yolks followed by the grated parmesan. Check the seasoning.
7. Spoon the lamb mixture into the base of a large ovenproof dish. Layer the mashed potato generously over the top, starting at the outside and working towards the middle.
8. Grate extra parmesan over the top and season. Fluff the mash with a fork to create rough peaks.
9. Bake for approximately 19 minutes, until bubbling and golden brown.'
WHERE `id` = 1
  AND `remote_url` = 'https://www.thecoast.net.nz/life/food-recipes/gordon-ramsay-s-shepherds-pie/'
  AND (`method` IS NULL OR TRIM(`method`) = '');

-- 10  Spaghetti and Meatballs
--     https://natashaskitchen.com/spaghetti-and-meatballs-recipe/
UPDATE `recipe` SET `method` = '1. Combine the bread with the water and leave for 5 minutes, then mash with a fork.
2. In a large bowl, combine the beef mince, sausage, grated parmesan, 4 cloves garlic, egg, and mashed bread mixture. Mix until well combined.
3. Form the mixture into meatballs about 4 cm wide. Roll lightly in flour and shake off any excess.
4. Heat oil in a deep large skillet or Dutch oven over medium heat. Brown the meatballs in 2 batches, turning until browned on all sides, about 6 minutes total. Remove and set aside.
5. In the same skillet, sauté the onion over medium heat until soft and golden, about 5 minutes. Add the remaining 4 cloves garlic and cook for 1 to 2 minutes.
6. Stir in the crushed tomato and bay leaves. Bring to a light boil.
7. Return the meatballs to the sauce, partially cover, and simmer gently for 30 minutes, turning occasionally. Stir in the basil a few minutes before the sauce is done, then season to taste.
8. Meanwhile, cook the spaghetti in boiling water according to the packet instructions until al dente. Drain and return to the pot.
9. Pour the sauce and meatballs over the spaghetti and toss gently. Garnish with extra parmesan and basil if desired, then serve hot.'
