UPDATE `recipe` SET `method` = '1. Trim excess silver skin and fat from the short ribs.
2. Season the ribs generously with salt and black pepper.
3. Peel and chop the carrots, slice the onions, and chop the celery into large chunks.
4. Heat the vegetable oil in a large Dutch oven over medium-high heat until smoking.
5. Brown the ribs in batches on all sides, without overcrowding the pot. Return each rib to the same spot to avoid burning the fond. If the fond is exposed, add onions to protect it from burning.
6. Remove the browned ribs and set aside. Add the onions and sauté until soft and browned, about 5-7 minutes. Add the tomato paste and sauté for 2-3 minutes, until slightly darkened.
7. Add the carrots and celery and sauté briefly to warm through.
8. Add the garlic, ale or wine, chicken or beef stock, soy sauce, and prune juice. Stir to combine, then add the thyme and parsley sprigs.
9. Nestle the ribs back into the pot with their least-browned sides facing up. The liquid should cover most of the ribs while leaving their tops slightly exposed.
10. Preheat the oven to 135°C. Cover the pot and braise for 4-5 hours, checking after 3.5 hours. The ribs are ready when easily pierced with a paring knife.
11. Remove the ribs and strain the braising liquid. Skim away excess fat if serving immediately. If making ahead, cool the liquid quickly with ice cubes, chill for at least 4 hours, then remove the solidified fat.
12. Return the ribs to the reduced sauce and reheat for 20 minutes. Baste with sauce, cover, and allow the ribs to rest briefly to absorb the flavours.
